---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";
import Tag from "../../components/docs/Tag.astro";

const replay = `coding.sh eval new latest --verifier 'npm test' --hidden-test 'test/retry.spec.ts'
coding.sh eval validate <task> --k 5
coding.sh eval export <task> --out ./tasks/retry --format harbor
coding.sh harness snapshot --agent claude-code --name base
coding.sh compare base candidate --k 3`;

const esc = (s: string) => s.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;");
const m = (s: string) => `<span class="t-muted">${esc(s)}</span>`;
const t = (s: string) => `<span class="t-text">${esc(s)}</span>`;
const ok = (s: string) => `<span class="t-pass">${esc(s)}</span>`;
const no = (s: string) => `<span class="t-fail">${esc(s)}</span>`;

// Real output of scripts/e2e/exp-ab-capture.mjs (2026-11-07): three Claude Code sessions against a stub
// model.
const AB = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh exp ab")}`,
  `A: git:HEAD   B: your edits   model: claude-sonnet-5-5`,
  `3 decision points from 3 sessions | A 0/3 -> B ${ok("3/3")} | fixed 3, broke 0 | paired p=0.25 | <$0.01, 0/6 cached`,
  `Hold: not enough evidence (B wins 3, loses 0; needs 8+ wins with no losses)`,
  `Replay noise floor 0% (A's turn differs from the recorded one in 0/3); B moved +100 points, above it`,
  "",
  `Fixed (A failed, B passed):`,
  `  unverified-claim "Make pad.js pad on the left."  ${m("coding.sh show 8f0e1e52-bdc1-4da0-a03b-14edf6dfcf77")}`,
  `  unverified-claim "Make clamp.js clamp to the upper bound too."  ${m("coding.sh show 9c1792df-6267-4b30-9f24-7a7307740543")}`,
  `  unverified-claim "Make range.js include its end value."  ${m("coding.sh show fe48a450-8867-48ec-b946-3b4d0cb6c780")}`,
  "",
  `Full comparison: coding.sh exp compare exp_01M6VXZSFPM2QK12ANN6Z269WP exp_01M6VXZSFSZ25HD2HXXVY40BXV`,
].join("\n");

// Real output of scripts/e2e/refine-loop.mjs with its CLAUDE.md named AGENTS.md and the draft at ./drafts/AGENTS.md
// (run 2026-11-09 against a stub model and a throwaway HOME).
// With CLAUDE.md the same run prints r_3123c2cbef and r_b25de6ab68; nothing else differs.
const REFINE = [
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh refine test ./drafts/AGENTS.md --behavior unchanged-retry")}`,
  `r_3b577b3897  ./drafts/AGENTS.md (AGENTS.md; +1 line)`,
  `  protected  ${ok("ok")} (1 protected line in place)`,
  `  critics    ${ok("ok")}`,
  `  dev        8 decision points | effect +100pp [+100pp, +100pp] | B wins 8, loses 0; A solved 0`,
  `  guard      8 tasks earlier kept edits won (0 from this run, 8 rerun) | ${no("forgets kept edit r_e67f549cae")}`,
  `  preserved  gained 8, lost 0, 0 tasks regressed`,
  `  verdict    ${no("reject")}: dev: Ship it: B wins 8 decision points, loses 0; guard: forgets kept edit r_e67f549cae`,
  "",
  `${m("$")} ${t("coding.sh refine log")}`,
  m(`ROUND  CANDIDATE     VERDICT        EFFECT (95% CI)          GUARD                 COST   REASON`),
  `1      r_e67f549cae  ${ok("keep, applied")}  +100pp [+100pp, +100pp]  no tasks              $0.02  dev: Ship it: B wins 8 decision points, loses 0`,
  `2      r_3b577b3897  ${no("reject")}         +100pp [+100pp, +100pp]  forgets r_e67f549cae  $0.02  guard: forgets kept edit r_e67f549cae`,
].join("\n");
const TRIGGER = [
  `${m("$")} ${t('coding.sh eval trigger skills/pdf --description "Use when the user mentions pdf, invoice, scanned." --yes')}`,
  `A (current): pdf on claude-code, k=1`,
  `  precision 67%  recall 50%  F1 0.57  accuracy 63%`,
  `  missed:`,
  `    should     loaded 0/1  "Make the invoice searchable"`,
  `    should_not false trigger 1/1  "Explain in one line what a pdf is"`,
  `  expected routes: docx 1/1`,
  `B (--description): pdf on claude-code, k=1`,
  `  precision 80%  recall 100%  F1 0.89  accuracy 88%`,
  "",
  `B vs A over 8 prompts: recall 2/4→4/4 · false triggers 1/4→1/4 · Δacc +25 pp (95% CI +0 to +63)`,
  `  flip ${ok("better")} should     skipped → loaded  "Make the invoice searchable"`,
  `  flip ${ok("better")} should     skipped → loaded  "OCR this scanned invoice"`,
  `  ${m("note: 4 should and 4 should_not prompts: only a large change can show; aim for 10+ per side")}`,
  `No clear difference`,
].join("\n");
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    The harness is everything around the model: AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md, rules, skills, the agent and its flags. These commands test a
    change to it before you keep it, on the sessions you already have or on tasks replayed from your repo.
  </p>

  <h2 id="exp-ab">A/B a harness edit on your sessions <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    You changed a line in AGENTS.md and want to know if it helps before you commit it. <code>coding.sh exp ab</code> answers that from
    the sessions you already have, with no eval tasks to write. It finds the moments in your recent sessions where the agent went wrong:
    it said it was done without running anything, ended a turn while its tests were failing, or retried a failed command unchanged. It
    replays the next turn at each of those moments twice, once with your committed files and once with your edit, and a judge scores both
    turns.
  </p>
  <Terminal title="~/repo" label="Output of coding.sh exp ab">
    <Fragment set:html={AB} />
  </Terminal>
  <p>
    The verdict line is the same ship gate <code>compare --gate</code> uses, and <code>--gate</code> makes it the exit code. The noise floor
    comes from the control arm: how often your committed files alone produce a different turn from the one recorded in the session. A
    difference smaller than that may just be replay noise. Use <code>--dry-run</code> to see the cases and a cost estimate before anything is
    called, and <code>--max-cost</code> to cap the spend. Responses are cached, so rerunning with unchanged files is free.
    <code>--watch</code> reruns each time you save, one line per run. See
    <a href="/docs/how-to/test-agents-md-change">Test an AGENTS.md change in five minutes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="protected">Protected safety lines <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    A pass-rate A/B can't see the harm of dropping a safety rule: if no case needed "Never run <code>git push --force</code>", deleting it
    costs nothing, and neither does rewording it into "only ask when the path is ambiguous". So <code>coding.sh refine check</code> checks a
    harness edit by text (default: git <code>HEAD</code> against your working tree) and exits 1 when it deletes or rewords a protected line
    (<code>protected-line</code>) or adds an exception that relaxes one (<code>relaxes-protected</code>). <code>exp ab</code> runs the same check
    on its arms, warns before any model call, and with <code>--gate</code> fails the run.
  </p>
  <p>
    A line is protected inside a <code>&lt;!-- coding.sh:protect --&gt;</code> block, when it matches a <code>[refine] protect</code> regex, or
    when one sentence pairs a gate word (never, don't, must not; or only, ask, confirm, verify at its start) with a safety topic
    (credentials, secrets, <code>.env</code>, prod, deploy, push, <code>--force</code>, delete, <code>rm -rf</code>, migrations, owner, …).
    <code>coding.sh refine protected</code> lists them. <code>--allow-protected-edit AGENTS.md:3</code> allows one edit on purpose. See
    <a href="https://github.com/jackulau/coding.sh/blob/main/docs/guides/evals.md#protected-safety-lines">Protected safety lines</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="refine">Refine loop <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    <code>coding.sh refine</code> improves AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, rules and skills in tested rounds. <code>refine propose</code> asks a model
    for lines that address your sessions' most severe flags, with the file's protected lines marked READ-ONLY and earlier rejected edits listed.
    You can also write the edit yourself. <code>refine test</code> then decides each candidate in stages: the protected-line check and two
    critics on the added lines (harmful instructions, near duplicates) before any model call, then an A/B against your current files on the
    decision points <code>exp ab</code> mines, through the same ship gate. Last comes the guard: the decision points earlier kept edits won must
    not go from solved to unsolved.
  </p>
  <Terminal title="~/repo" label="Output of coding.sh refine test and refine log">
    <Fragment set:html={REFINE} />
  </Terminal>
  <p>
    Here the second edit wins its own decision points but drops the line the first edit added, so the guard rejects it.
    <code>refine apply</code> writes a kept candidate with a backup and never commits. <code>refine rollback</code> restores the backup byte for
    byte. <code>refine log</code> shows every round with its verdict, effect CI, guard result, cost and reason. <code>refine run</code> loops
    propose, test and apply until <code>--rounds</code>, <code>--max-cost</code> or the plateau detector says stop. <code>--dry-run</code>
    shows the plan, the guard size and the cost first. See
    <a href="https://github.com/jackulau/coding.sh/blob/main/docs/guides/evals.md#refine-loop">Refine loop</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="triggers">Skill triggers <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    A skill only helps when the agent loads it. <code>coding.sh eval trigger &lt;skill-dir&gt;</code> reads a <code>triggers.toml</code> next to
    the skill with <code>[[should]]</code> prompts the skill is for and <code>[[should_not]]</code> near misses. It runs each prompt k times in a
    fresh empty directory with the skill and its sibling skills installed, and reads from each captured session whether the agent loaded the
    skill. It reports precision, recall, F1 and Wilson intervals, and lists the prompts it missed or loaded by mistake. A near miss can name the
    sibling that should take it (<code>expect_skill</code>). <code>--description</code> or <code>--candidate DIR</code> runs a second arm with the
    same prompts in the same order and adds a paired bootstrap CI on accuracy and the prompts that flipped.
  </p>
  <Terminal title="~/repo" label="Output of coding.sh eval trigger with a description A/B">
    <Fragment set:html={TRIGGER} />
  </Terminal>
  <p>
    The output above is the CI check with real Claude Code against a stub model. Eight prompts can't show a change of this size, so the verdict
    holds. <code>--min-f1</code> and <code>--fail-on regression</code> set the exit code for CI. See
    <a href="https://github.com/jackulau/coding.sh/blob/main/docs/how-to/evaluate-a-skill.md#does-the-skill-load">Does the skill load?</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="repo-replay">Repo-replay tasks <Tag status="partial" /></h2>
  <p>
    For coding agents, a task is the repository snapshot from before the agent ran, the first prompt as the instruction, setup commands and a
    verifier command. Before a task counts, <code>eval validate</code> checks the verifier:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Doing nothing fails.</li>
    <li>The reference solution (the session's own diff, or a later fix) passes <code>k</code> runs out of <code>k</code> (default 5), so flaky tests fail validation.</li>
    <li>Near-miss variants of the reference, generated by mutating its patch, fail.</li>
    <li>Nothing leaks: hidden test files are copied in only when the verifier runs, and follow-up prompts that steer toward the answer stay out of the instruction.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    It also flags gold-only coupling: a hidden test that names a helper or file only the reference adds, when the instruction never mentions
    it. A valid fix that names things differently would fail. These show as notes; <code>--strict-coupling</code> makes them fail validation.
    <code>eval crosscheck &lt;task&gt;</code> works from the other side: a reviewer model reads the instruction and each stored run's diff and
    says whether the change is valid. Where it disagrees with the verifier, it reports a false reject or a false accept, and stores the counts
    on the task in the same shape as a human verifier audit. <code>--solver &lt;bundle&gt;</code> solves the task once first, and
    <code>--max</code> caps the model calls (default 5).
  </p>
  <p id="witness">
    <code>eval audit &lt;task&gt; --experiment &lt;id&gt; --witness &lt;file&gt; --witness-cmd &lt;cmd&gt;</code> checks passing runs with a
    test for a requirement the verifier skips. The witness must pass the reference 3/3; then every passing run is rebuilt from its diff, the
    verifier must still pass, and a witness that fails twice in a row refutes the run:
    <code>claude-tdd: passes 6/10, refuted 3, audited pass rate 30% (3/10)</code>. A witness refuting over half the passes is ambiguous
    and not counted. No model calls (TestJack, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.10619">arXiv 2610.10619</a>).
    <code>--promote</code> adds the witness to the verifier.
  </p>
  <p>
    Harness bundles (agent, model, flags, env and files such as <code>AGENTS.md</code> or <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, identified by a content hash)
    are what <code>compare</code> A/B tests: it runs both bundles on validated tasks in isolated worktrees and reports per-arm Wilson
    intervals, a paired bootstrap interval, McNemar's test, cost, token, turn and latency deltas, and the first step where the two traces diverge. Tasks export
    in coding.sh's own layout or Harbor's.
  </p>
  <p id="gate">
    <code>compare A B --gate</code> answers "should I ship B?" in one line: <code>Ship it: B wins 9 tasks, loses 0</code>,
    <code>Hold: not enough evidence</code>, or <code>Don't ship: B breaks 3 tasks A solved</code>, with the broken tasks listed. It exits 0
    only for "Ship it". A break costs two fixes (<code>--lambda</code>), the wins must clear a one-sided exact test (<code>--gate-alpha</code>,
    default 0.05), and B may break at most 10% of the tasks A solved (<code>--tau</code>). This is the SAGE accept-or-abstain rule
    (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36043">arXiv 2609.36043</a>). Passes that tampered with tests count as fails for the gate.
    <code>--gate --by suite:</code> also gates each group of tasks (by their first tag with that prefix), so a pooled "Ship it" can't hide
    one suite collapsing: any group's "Don't ship" blocks. The report also splits each arm's failed runs by the agent's own last test run
    (<code>A fails 12: own tests green 7 (58%), red 3, stale 1, none 1</code>): green fails are silent misses, where the agent's tests passed
    and the verifier's did not.
  </p>
  <p id="tamper">
    Passing runs that edited, deleted or skipped tests or the verifier's files still count as passes, so compare links each such change to
    the tool call that made it and splits those passes: <code>tampered passes B 3: disclosed 2, stealth 1</code>. Disclosed means the agent
    said it was changing the test before or with the call; stealth means it didn't (EPISTEME,
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06563">arXiv 2611.06563</a>). Each pass gets a line with the run, file, tool, event id and the
    agent's words; <code>--json</code> has them under <code>tamperAttribution</code>.
  </p>
  <p id="audited">
    After <a href="#witness"><code>eval audit</code></a>, compare shows each audited arm's raw and audited pass rate:
    <code>audited pass B 60% → 30% (3 refuted, coverage 100%)</code>. <code>compare A B --audited</code> counts refuted passes as fails
    and labels the verdict <code>verdict (audited)</code>, with a note when that changes it. It needs every pass in both arms replayed;
    otherwise the raw verdict stands and a note says what the audit covers.
  </p>
  <p id="promote">
    <code>compare A B --promote</code> keeps a B that is cheaper at the same quality, which the verdict calls "no significant difference". It
    prints <code>pareto: support 0.86 ≥ 0.70 → promote (cost-cut: cost -38% …)</code> or <code>→ hold</code>, and exits 0 only on promote.
    The support is the share of bootstrap draws (over tasks and attempts) where B gains quality without costing more than
    <code>--eps-c</code>, or costs less while losing at most <code>--eps-q</code> of pass rate. Cost is USD, or tokens when too few runs are
    priced. Method: ParetoSaddler (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02079">arXiv 2611.02079</a>).
  </p>
  <p id="item-gate">
    When B adds or removes one rule, skill or context file and nothing else (<code>--without</code> counts), the report adds a one-item gate:
    <code>item gate k=3 τ=.34 m=2: KEEP · G=1.67 net +4 runs D=.33 fired 11/36 · S=9 tasks</code>. It scores only the tasks where the item
    reached the agent's context, read from each run's trace. An addition is KEEP, RECHECK (re-run the tasks that dropped), KILL or RERUN; a
    removal is ACCEPT (only when the agent passes at least 80% of the exposed tasks without it), REJECT, UNMEASURED (it never fired, or
    the pass rate without it is under 80%, so keep it), UNCOVERED (neither arm solves the tasks: repair the item or check the tasks) or RERUN. <code>--item-gate</code> makes it set the exit code. Method:
    Cortex (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14216">arXiv 2610.14216</a>).
  </p>
  <p id="plugins">
    A claude-code bundle can carry Claude Code plugins, the whole plugin directory: <code>harness snapshot --agent claude-code --plugin terse</code>
    (an installed name, <code>name@marketplace</code> or a plugin directory; repeatable), or <code>plugins = [...]</code> in a harness TOML.
    <code>compare A --with plugin:terse</code> runs A against A plus the plugin in one command, and <code>--without plugin:&lt;name&gt;</code>
    drops one. Each run loads the plugin with <code>--plugin-dir</code> from its own home; your Claude config is never changed.
  </p>
  <p id="hand-written">
    A task can also be written by hand: a directory with <code>task.json</code>, <code>instruction.md</code>, an optional <code>repo/</code>
    start tree and <code>tests/</code> for hidden files. <code>eval import tasks/*/</code> loads them as drafts, and
    <code>compare A B --tag &lt;tag&gt;</code> runs them. The verifier gets the agent's final answer and its whole stdout as files
    (<code>CODING_SH_AGENT_OUTPUT</code>, <code>CODING_SH_AGENT_STDOUT</code>), so <code>judge grade --grader checklist.json</code> can grade
    what the agent said and which commands it ran. <code>--max-cost 15</code> stops starting runs once agent and judge spend reach $15;
    the report shows both as <code>agent spend</code> and <code>grader spend</code>. The <a href="/docs/how-to/evaluate-a-skill">Evaluate an
    Agent Skill</a> guide walks through it.
  </p>
  <p id="reuse">
    <code>compare A B --reuse baseline</code> copies A's finished runs from earlier comparisons instead of running them again, so comparing
    several candidates against one baseline pays for the baseline once. A run is reused only when its task (instruction, verifier, hidden
    files), bundle, agent version, attempt and timeout are all the same, keyed by content, and only when it finished without an error inside
    <code>--reuse-max-age</code> (default 7 days). <code>--dry-run</code> prints how many runs each arm would reuse and run. The report notes
    that reused runs did not run interleaved with the new ones.
  </p>
  <p id="release">
    Each run records the agent release it started on, checked at most once a minute, so an auto-update mid-comparison shows. When the arms
    ran on different releases, the report adds <code>agent claude-code 2.1.316 ×12 2.1.319 ×12 release differs</code> and a note that
    the delta mixes the change with the release. <code>--gate</code> repeats it, and <code>--promote</code> holds and exits 1
    (<code>pareto.refused: "release_confound"</code>). <code>--json</code> has <code>report.agentVersions</code>. Releases change the
    agent's own prompt and tools often (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37332">arXiv 2610.37332</a>).
  </p>
  <Code code={replay} />
  <p id="iterate">
    <code>--experiment exp_01 --retry-errors</code> runs again, in place, the runs of a stored comparison that errored or failed on
    infrastructure; the old runs stay, marked superseded, so each task, arm and attempt counts once. <code>--failing exp_01</code> runs
    only the tasks A failed in <code>exp_01</code> (<code>exp_01@bundle</code> for another arm). The difference holds on those tasks, but both
    pass rates are conditioned on A having failed them, and the report says so. <code>--sample 20 --seed 7</code> runs the same random
    20 tasks for the same seed.
  </p>
  <p id="repair">
    <code>compare A B --repair 2</code> also measures how well the agent fixes its own failure. When the verifier fails, the agent gets
    "Verification failed." in its own session (Claude Code <code>--resume</code>, <code>codex exec resume</code>) and tries again, up to 2
    rounds. The hidden tests are out of the work tree while it works. <code>--repair-signal names</code> adds the failing test names read from
    the verifier output. A round that edits, deletes or skips tests does not count as a repair. <code>--false-alarm 0.2</code> tells a
    seeded 20% of passing runs they failed, to measure what a wrong signal breaks. The report adds, per arm, Seq@k (the share of tasks
    solved by the k-th attempt), the repair rate, where the gain lands (EGS, LGS), rounds that changed nothing, cost to solve and, with
    <code>--k 2</code> or more, a cost front against k independent attempts. <code>--repair-verdict</code> moves the verdict to the
    outcome after repair.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Known gap.</strong> A hook-captured session starts from the commit that was checked out when it began, so uncommitted changes at
      that point are missing and <code>eval new</code> warns. <code>coding.sh run --</code> snapshots the whole working tree before and after,
      and takes the instruction from the command's prompt argument, or from <code>--instruction</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
